rsz: recompute design area for a progress row only after an accepted move

Candidate fix from the repair_timing runtime study; not upstreamed.
Retires at the bump onto an OpenROAD that carries it or a better
version of it.

Every -verbose progress row calls computeDesignArea(), a walk over every
instance in the block. Most rows follow passes that changed nothing --
on riscv32i cts 217 of 1181 passes accepted a move -- so the area is
recomputed only when the committer's accepted-move count has changed
since the row that last computed it.

Chesterton's Fence: Resizer::design_area_ exists and is meant to be
incremental, but designArea() re-derives it on every call because
buffer insertion does not go through designAreaIncr(), so the tracked
value cannot be trusted. Making it trustworthy is a larger change across
every instance-creating path; caching by move count is the local step
that removes the per-row cost without touching that.

